model {
  extend basalt.modelGateway {
    metadata {
      constraint "A fleet row whose count no stream can lower, because its releases were lost with an object's isolate, never retires by itself. The signed fleet-retire command, sent by the fleet lane's retire-row mode under a coordinator GO and naming the row by its key, ends it. It refuses unless the key names exactly one row, that row is draining, never ready, and its count stood still for the leaked-streams bound of 300 s in the controller's ledger. The cell then refuses too unless its own count is draining and known, and, while it isn't already 0, stood still as long and holds no lease its current isolate took, since such a stream may still be live. It reads its established connections on 8317 as evidence only, releases the still count, honours the caller's abort before committing, and logs a closed POOL_RUNTIME_STILL_RELEASED line. Once the cell has answered, the controller lowers the ledger to 0 only while the ledger is still what it read, otherwise upkeep's reconcile lowers it, and logs one closed POOL_FLEET_ROW_RETIRE_OVERRIDE line whether or not the ledger step succeeded, naming the GO, the row key, both overridden counts with how long each stood, whether the ledger was lowered, and the connection reading. Upkeep's ordinary retirement then stops the cell and holds its place until the provider proves the instance gone. A model socket the cell passes through natively can outlive the isolate that took its lease, so it can still be cut; the 8317 reading is the evidence that none is open, the lane warns when it isn't 0, and the command takes a GO and acts only on a draining row."
    }
  }
}
